Subject: [PATCH 2/2] AArch64 peephole: store literal zero via wzr/xzr (audited
 lev 86b34508 port)

Storing a constant zero materialized a register first (mov x0, #0;
strb w0, [x9]); the zero register does it for free (strb wzr, [x9]).
The mov stays for dead-move elimination (it may have other readers).
sieve's marking loop now stores through wzr (3 sites); zero-init
patterns in histogram/hash_table benefit equally.

Audit deltas vs his commit:
- dst < 29 instead of his <= 30: x29/x30 (frame pointer, link register)
  are absurd zero-source candidates and deserve no code path.
- Verified the rewrite touches ONLY the store's VALUE operand: a store
  whose BASE register holds the zero temp breaks out (first != value
  operand match fails), pinned by the adversarial test (table[idx] with
  idx==0 keeps its base register).
- movn exclusion verified (#0 under movn is -1); multi-line slots
  skipped; scan stops at first barrier/clobber/load; stp first-operand
  rewrite covered by the same code path.

Wired into phase 1 and phase 3 after fold_destructive_pointer_updates
(his ordering; the destructive-update folds must win first).

Validation: 1075/1075 unit, 406/406 x86 regression, aarch64 fuzz
450/450 (O1/O2/Os seeds 0:150), ARM regression 242 pass / 0 new fails,
ARM benchmark outputs identical (sieve/histogram/ascii_case_fold/
hash_table re-verified post-change).
